Gov. Rick Perry today met with President Barack Obama to discuss the ongoing humanitarian and national security crises occurring along the Texas-Mexico border. In the meeting, Gov. Perry urged the president to undertake the necessary steps to secure the border and ease the crises.
"Five hundred miles south of here in the Rio Grande Valley there is a humanitarian crisis unfolding that has been created by bad public policy, in particular the failure to secure the border," Gov. Perry said. "Securing the border is attainable, and the president needs to commit the resources necessary to get this done."
Among the requests Gov. Perry made to President Obama to secure the border:
• Visit the Texas-Mexico border to witness firsthand the impact of the border crisis;
• Deploy an additional 1,000 National Guard troops to the Texas-Mexico border to immediately enhance border security operations;
• Direct the Federal Aviation Administration to allow the National Guard to utilize Predator drones along the Texas-Mexico border for identifying and tracking human and drug trafficking;
• Medically screen all illegal immigrants to ensure their health and the health of our citizens;
• Direct the Centers for Disease Control or another appropriate federal agency outside the Department of Homeland Security (DHS) to conduct, in conjunction with the Texas Department of State Health Services (DSHS), inspections of facilities in which illegal immigrants, including UACs, are being housed to ensure accepted international and national emergency sheltering standards are met;
• Modify or rescind policies that serve as a magnet to encourage illegal immigration, including:
• DHS Catch and Release policies by which DHS issues an illegal immigrant an NTA before an immigration judge and releases them. The U.S. Department of Justice reports that 33 percent of those released on their own recognizance failed to appear in FY 2013.
• Deportation policies and procedures that prohibit UACs from countries other than Mexico and Canada from being immediately repatriated back to their country of origin. After DHS processes these UACs, they are issued an NTA and delivered to a sponsor or relative in the United States, regardless of the relative's immigration status.
President Barack Obama tried to ratchet up pressure on Congress – and specifically Texas Republicans – to act quickly to provide resources to deal with the influx of unaccompanied children at the state’s southern border.Obama, in the state for three fundraisers for Democratic campaigns this fall, said that his administration would consider suggestions offered by Gov. Rick Perry in a private meeting, such as moving Border Patrol agents and increasing surveillance.“There’s nothing that the governor indicated he’d like to see that I have a philosophical objection to,” Obama said at Love Field after a larger meeting that included Perry, Dallas Mayor Mike Rawlings, Dallas County Judge Clay Jenkins and several other local officials and religious leaders.But the president noted that the White House had requested an emergency $3.7 billion from Congress and had encountered pushback from Republicans. He said he asked Perry to use his sway with fellow Texas Republicans, the largest GOP delegation in the House, to get the legislation moving quickly.One of the meeting attendees, Dallas County Commissioner Elba Garcia, called the meeting friendly.Obama and Perry were "cordial," she said. The talk lasted for a little over half an hour. The president listened to input from all the people involved, Garcia said, but the conversation didn't delve into many specific details.She said that Perry pushed for better border security, a need that Obama and the others in the room agreed about.Garcia said she didn't learn anything new about the plans for housing children in Dallas County. Those details still need to be ironed out, she said."It really was an exchange of ideas," she said. "I feel very pleased with the result."As President Obama was speaking, Gov. Rick Perry issued a press release about their meeting. It reads in full:Reports Gromer Jeffers Jr.: Before entering a closed-door meeting at DalFort Fueling in Love Field, President Barack Obama and Gov. Rick Perry had a meeting that lasted about 15 minutes.Afterward, Obama and Perry attended a meeting on the border crisis that included Dallas County Judge Clay Jenkins, Dallas Mayor Mike Rawlings, Dallas County Commissioner Elba Garcia, Rep. Eddie Bernice Johnson of Dallas, Grand Prairie Mayor Ron Jensen, Chris Liebrum of the Baptist General Convention, Arne Nelson of Catholic Charities, Texas Health and Human Services Director Kyle Janek and Texas Public Safety Director Steven McCraw.President Barack Obama is meeting now with Gov. Rick Perry, Dallas Mayor Mike Rawlings, Dallas County Judge Clay Jenkins, Grand Prairie Mayor Ron Jensen and other state and local officials and leaders at Dallas Love Field to discuss the border crisis. (Photographers were allowed to briefly snap pictures, but reporters are not able to cover the meeting.)The president is expected to emerge at about 5:45 p.m. to make a statement on the meeting. He'll then be whisked to the home of Dallas lawyer Marc Stanley for the first of two fundraisers, the second one slated for late tonight in Austin.Check back later for more on the president's statement and comments from other participants of the meeting.WASHINGTON -- Gov. Rick Perry's border summit with the president will take place in the most exclusive chopper in all the land, as Marine One whisks them across Dallas.Perry, one of Barack Obama's loudest antagonists, requested a meeting on Monday during the president's two-day Texas visit and pointedly pre-rejected any "quick handshake on the tarmac." White House spokesman Josh Earnest revealed moments ago that this would indeed be no pedestrian encounter."The president and Gov. Perry will board Marine One and it will fly to Dallas Love Field" from D/FW International Airport, where Air Force One will land shortly, Earnest told reporters during the flight from Denver.They'll get a chance to chat about the border crisis on their way to Love, where Obama will meet with Perry, Dallas Mayor Mike Rawlings, Dallas County Judge Clay Jenkins and Rep. Eddie Bernice Johnson, Earnest said.Until now the White House had kept the guest list a closely guarded secret. The event was hastily arranged; Johnson got her invitation yesterday."They disagree on many issues," Earnest said of Obama and Perry, but "If there is an opportunity... to set aside partisan labels and focus on solutions, then that’s what the president is going to do."He continued to deflect questions about the president’s decision not to visit the border while in Texas, the state seeing the brunt of the migration crisis."What the president is most focused on is providing the leadership" needed to solve the problem, he said.Obama sees common ground Perry on the need to boost surveillance resources at the border, Earnest said, noting that the president’s $3.7 billion request to Congress includes some funds for that.Earnest ticked off a number of recent developments showing, he said, how engaged Obama is in addressing the border crisis. A deputy attorney general is in McAllen today touring a border facility. The Justice Department announced earlier today that it will shift resources from the interior to the border, to speed processing of cases involving recently detained migrants.He also lauded the government of Mexico for taking steps to tighten security along its own Southern border, where the long and dangerous journey begins for many of the migrants.“This will allow the Mexicans to ramp up their efforts to tighten security….and crack down on human trafficking and other criminal networks,” he said.Vice President Biden also called leaders in Central America today -- Guatemalan President Otto Perez Molina, Honduran President Juan Orlando Hernandez, and El Salvadoran President Salvador Sanchez Ceren -- to discuss the crisis.“This administration, at the specific direction of the president, is moving forward with a sense of urgency on multiple fronts,” Earnest said.Gromer Jeffers, awaiting their arrival at Love, also spotted Dallas County Commissioner Elba Garcia.
